  "sections": [
    {
      "id": "ghanaian-traditions-tunes-forms-history-and-recordings",
      "title": "Ghanaian Traditions — Tunes — Forms, History, and Recordings",
      "level": 1,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "**Track:** West African Roots | **Region:** africa-ghana | **Tier:** 4.5\n**Prerequisites:** regions-africa-ghana-07-representative-depth\n**Tags:** africa, ghana, west-africa, tunes, —"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "overview",
      "title": "Overview",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Highlife listening\n\nGhanaian Traditions belongs to the **West African Roots** curriculum. E.T. Mensah\n\nThis lesson develops listening precision for **Highlife listening** — connecting repertoire, instruments, dance, and the institutions that transmit music today.\n\n![Traditional musicians](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Fiddle.jpg?width=640)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "compared-with-mali",
      "title": "Compared with Mali",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "**Mande Music** (`regions-africa-mali-08-tunes`) covers the same lesson slot from the anchor perspective. This lesson stresses: **Highlife listening**.\n\n| Topic | Mali anchor | Ghanaian Traditions (this lesson) |\n|-------|------------------------------|-------------------------------------|\n| Focus | Kaira, Mande repertoire… | Highlife listening… |\n\n**Parallel anchor lesson:** `regions-africa-mali-08-tunes`\n**Secondary comparison (Ireland):** `regions-celtic-ireland-08-tunes`"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "core-material",
      "title": "Core material",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Highlife listening\n\nStudy **ghanaian traditions** through listening first. Wikipedia's article on [Music of Ghana](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Music_of_Ghana) orients geography and history; recordings prove how musicians phrase ornaments, tempo, and ensemble balance in real rooms."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "tune-studies",
      "title": "Tune studies",
      "level": 3,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Representative repertoire includes **Highlife listening**. Use the lesson tune panel and **Play all** to compare multiple settings of the same title or form."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "regional-accent-and-social-context",
      "title": "Regional accent and social context",
      "level": 3,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Tradition lives in **community practice** — festivals, dances, teaching networks, and archives — not only on concert stages. For **West African Roots**, ask who leads (voice, fiddle, pipes, percussion), whether dance drives repertoire, and how revival institutions shaped what you hear on modern recordings."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "instruments-and-voices-in-this-lesson",
      "title": "Instruments and voices in this lesson",
      "level": 3,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Lead voices: **kora overlap, voice, percussion**. Ensemble layer: **ensemble**. Compare timbre and role with the anchor unit before assuming techniques transfer unchanged."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "forms-dance-and-rhythm",
      "title": "Forms, dance, and rhythm",
      "level": 3,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Named forms include **highlife roots, folk song**. Dance context: **social dance**. Metre and foot-tap feel are not decorative — they explain why musicians accent certain beats and phrase repeats."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "history-transmission-and-today",
      "title": "History, transmission, and today",
      "level": 3,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Historical threads: **colonial and post-colonial media**. Contemporary scenes: **highlife scenes**. Tune repertoire anchor: **Highlife listening**. Follow lesson playlists and reading lists for recorded examples."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "listening-habits",
      "title": "Listening habits",
      "level": 3,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "1. Queue one field or archive recording and one contemporary ensemble track.\n2. Log metre, lead instrument, ornament, and social context in four columns.\n3. Return to the parallel anchor lesson if comparison feels abstract.\n4. Avoid generic world-music compilations when building ear training."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "study-facts",
      "title": "Study facts",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "- Highlife listening\n- Lead instruments: kora overlap, voice, percussion\n- Ensemble: ensemble\n- Forms: highlife roots, folk song\n- Dance: social dance\n- History: colonial and post-colonial media\n- Scenes: highlife scenes\n- Repertoire: Highlife listening\n- Compared with Mali: regional accent and social context differ.\n- Revival movements and media shaped twentieth-century practice.\n- Dance rhythm and tune metre are linked in this tradition."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "key-points",
      "title": "Key points",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "- Ghanaian Traditions is a living tradition with regional accent.\n- Focus: Highlife listening.\n- Compared with Mali: shared heritage, distinct emphasis.\n- Forms: highlife roots, folk song.\n- Dance: social dance.\n- Transmission: colonial and post-colonial media."
        }
      ]
    },
